One way to explain this decision is as follows. Looking at the current threat levels and the number of drones needed for full protection, Field_1 (threat 0.23) and Field_3 (threat 0.17) are the highest‐risk fields. Even though the present setup has Field_2 fully protected (4 drones) and Field_4 nearly so (2 out of 3), leaving Field_1 unprotected would be very costly. Since a fully protected field causes birds to immediately flee, it is far more effective than a partial protection (which only reduces the damage somewhat). 

Given that we have only 8 drones, the “optimal” grouping in terms of minimizing overall damage is to concentrate our available assets on fully securing the two highest‐threat fields (Field_1 and Field_3). Field_1 requires 4 drones and Field_3 requires 2 drones, consuming 6 drones in all. The remaining 2 drones are then assigned to Field_2 (threat 0.12), even though 4 would be needed for full protection. Field_4 (threat 0.10) is left without drone protection, but its lower risk makes this the better trade–off. 

Thus, the groups become:
• The 4 drones (Drone_1, Drone_4, Drone_5 and Drone_7) go to Field_1 to fully protect it.
• Drone_6 and Drone_8 remain protecting Field_3 (which already requires and has exactly 2 drones).
• Drone_2 and Drone_3 are reassigned to Field_2 – while this leaves Field_2 partially protected, it is acceptable given its lower threat compared to Field_1.
• Field_4 ends up with no drones.
• No drones remain idle.

This assignment strikes a balance in minimizing the overall damage by fully securing the fields where birds would inflict the most harm.
