One way to explain this decision is as follows. Although initially the “protecting‐group” assignments were set up so that Field_2 (with a low threat level of 0.05) had four drones, Field_3 (highest threat 0.20) was fully covered by two drones and Field_4 (threat 0.06) had two drones (short of its requirement of three), Field_1 (with a moderately high threat level of 0.15 and needing four drones) was left without protection.

Because only a field that is “fully protected” makes the birds flee, and because partial protection has very little effect, it is advantageous to use our limited fleet on the fields that matter most. In a “reallocation‐by‐efficiency” approach the idea is to secure the fields with the highest threat per protection–unit. Field_3 is already secured, so our 8 drones can be reassigned so that the field with the next‐highest threat (Field_1) is fully protected. Since Field_2’s threat is very low (0.05), it is a good candidate to sacrifice. In other words, we take the 4 drones that were originally protecting Field_2 and assign them instead to Field_1. This yields full protection at Field_1 and Field_3, leaves Field_2 unprotected (but with low threat) and leaves Field_4 with its current two drones (still partially protected). Overall this grouping is expected to reduce potential damage compared to leaving Field_1 unprotected while Field_2 was fully covered.

The grouping printed above is one valid assignment that meets the “every drone in exactly one group” requirement and that follows this line of reasoning.
